3D matter–geometry state

Instead of scalars, treat both as 3D fields on a grid.

- Geometry field  
  \[
  g(\mathbf{x}, t) \rightarrow \text{e.g. scalar curvature field or tensor components per cell}
  \]  
  Represented as a 3D array: g.shape = (Nx, Ny, Nz, Gd)  
  where Gd = number of geometry components (e.g. 1 for scalar, 6 for symmetric tensor).

- Matter field  
  \[
  M(\mathbf{x}, t) \rightarrow \text{e.g. density, pressure, field values}
  \]  
  Represented as: M.shape = (Nx, Ny, Nz, Md)  
  where Md = number of matter components.

State:

`python
g: np.ndarray  # (Nx, Ny, Nz, Gd)
M: np.ndarray  # (Nx, Ny, Nz, Md)
state = (g, M)
`

